## Run

Start the development server:

```sh
uv run fastapi dev src/{{ spec.package }}/main.py
```

The API is available at <http://127.0.0.1:8000>. Interactive documentation is
served at <http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs>.
{% if spec.has("sqlalchemy") %}

## Item endpoints

| Method | Path | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/items` | Create an item and return it with status `201`. |
| `GET` | `/items` | List items ordered by id. |
| `GET` | `/items/{item_id}` | Return one item or `404`. |
| `PATCH` | `/items/{item_id}` | Update the provided fields or return `404`. |
| `DELETE` | `/items/{item_id}` | Delete one item and return `204`, or `404`. |

List requests accept `limit` (1 to 100, default 50) and `offset` (0 or greater,
default 0):

```sh
curl "http://127.0.0.1:8000/items?limit=20&offset=0"
```

Create and update requests use JSON bodies:

```sh
curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/items" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name": "first", "description": "example"}'
```
{% endif %}
{% if spec.has("auth-jwt") %}

## Authentication endpoints

| Method | Path | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/auth/register` | Register a user and return it with status `201`. |
| `POST` | `/auth/login` | Accept form fields `username` and `password` and return a bearer token. |
| `GET` | `/auth/me` | Return the authenticated user or `401`. |

The application reads the JWT signing key from `SECRET_KEY`. The value in
`.env.example` is only a placeholder; replace it with your own secret before
deploying. `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` controls the token lifetime and defaults
to `30`.

Register a user, request a token, and call the protected route:

```sh
curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/register" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user@example.com", "password": "correct-horse-battery"}'

curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/login" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -d "username=user@example.com&password=correct-horse-battery"

curl "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/me" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <access_token>"
```
{% endif %}
{% if spec.has("email") %}

## Email, password reset, and verification

Email messages are rendered from `templates/email/` with Jinja2. The
`EMAIL_BACKEND` setting selects the transport: `console` (the default) writes
messages to standard output for local development, while `smtp` sends them
through the configured SMTP server. The console backend prints the rendered
message for local use and never prints SMTP credentials.

| Method | Path | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST` | `/auth/request-password-reset` | Send a reset link token when the email is registered and return `202`. |
| `POST` | `/auth/reset-password` | Accept `token` and `new_password` and return `204`, or `400` for an invalid token. |
| `POST` | `/auth/request-verification` | Send a verification token when the email is registered and return `202`. |
| `POST` | `/auth/verify-email` | Accept `token`, mark the email as verified, and return `204`, or `400` for an invalid token. |

Requests for an unknown email return the same generic `202` response as a
registered email and send nothing. Password reset tokens expire after 30
minutes; email verification tokens expire after 24 hours.

Request a reset and submit the token shown by the console backend:

```sh
curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/request-password-reset" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email": "user@example.com"}'

curl -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:8000/auth/reset-password" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"token": "<token>", "new_password": "new-correct-horse-battery"}'
```

Configure SMTP with `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, `SMTP_PASSWORD`,
`SMTP_FROM`, and `SMTP_STARTTLS`. The values in `.env.example` are local
placeholders; replace them before deploying. Set `EMAIL_BACKEND=smtp` to use
the SMTP backend.
{% endif %}
